#### ADB Device Instance Caching
```java
// Cache ADB device instances with TTL (in Emulator class)
private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, IDevice> deviceCache = 
    new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, Long> deviceCacheTimestamp = 
    new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
private static final long DEVICE_CACHE_TTL = 30000; // 30 seconds

protected IDevice findDevice(String emulatorNumber) {
    IDevice cached = deviceCache.get(emulatorNumber);
    Long timestamp = deviceCacheTimestamp.get(emulatorNumber);
    
    if (cached != null && timestamp != null) {
        long age = System.currentTimeMillis() - timestamp;
        if (age < DEVICE_CACHE_TTL) {
            return cached;
        }
    }
    
    // Refresh cache
    IDevice device = lookupDevice(emulatorNumber);
    deviceCache.put(emulatorNumber, device);
    deviceCacheTimestamp.put(emulatorNumber, System.currentTimeMillis());
    
    return device;
}
```

#### Service Singleton Pattern
```java
// Services are singletons, manage resources carefully
public class ServScheduler {
    private static ServScheduler instance;
    private final Map<String, TaskQueue> queues = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    
    public static ServScheduler getServices() {
        if (instance == null) {
            synchronized (ServScheduler.class) {
                if (instance == null) {
                    instance = new ServScheduler();
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
    
    // Cleanup on shutdown
    public void shutdown() {
        queues.values().forEach(TaskQueue::stop);
        queues.clear();
    }
}
```

### 8. ThreadLocal Memory Management

#### Proper ThreadLocal Usage
```java
public class ThreadLocalManager {
    private static final ThreadLocal<UserContext> userContext = new ThreadLocal<>();
    private static final ThreadLocal<DateFormat> dateFormat = 
        ThreadLocal.withInitial(() -> new SimpleDateFormat("yyyy-MM-dd"));
    
    public static void setUserContext(UserContext context) {
        userContext.set(context);
    }
    
    public static UserContext getUserContext() {
        return userContext.get();
    }
    
    // Critical: Always clean up ThreadLocal
    public static void clearUserContext() {
        userContext.remove();
    }
    
    public static void clearDateFormat() {
        dateFormat.remove();
    }
    
    // Use in web applications with filters
    @Component
    public class ThreadLocalCleanupFilter implements Filter {
        @Override
        public void doFilter(ServletRequest request, ServletResponse response, 
                           FilterChain chain) throws IOException, ServletException {
            try {
                chain.doFilter(request, response);
            } finally {
                ThreadLocalManager.clearUserContext();
                ThreadLocalManager.clearDateFormat();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### 9. Memory Monitoring and Diagnostics

#### JVM Memory Monitoring
```java
@Component
public class MemoryMonitor {
    private final MBeanServer mBeanServer = ManagementFactory.getPlatformMBeanServer();
    
    @Scheduled(fixedRate = 60000) // Every minute
    public void logMemoryUsage() {
        MemoryMXBean memoryBean = ManagementFactory.getMemoryMXBean();
        MemoryUsage heapUsage = memoryBean.getHeapMemoryUsage();
        MemoryUsage nonHeapUsage = memoryBean.getNonHeapMemoryUsage();
        
        log.info("Heap Memory: Used={}, Max={}, Usage={}%", 
            heapUsage.getUsed() / 1024 / 1024,
            heapUsage.getMax() / 1024 / 1024,
            (heapUsage.getUsed() * 100.0) / heapUsage.getMax());
        
        log.info("Non-Heap Memory: Used={}, Max={}", 
            nonHeapUsage.getUsed() / 1024 / 1024,
            nonHeapUsage.getMax() / 1024 / 1024);
        
        // GC information
        List<GarbageCollectorMXBean> gcBeans = ManagementFactory.getGarbageCollectorMXBeans();
        for (GarbageCollectorMXBean gcBean : gcBeans) {
            log.info("GC {}: Collections={}, Time={}ms", 
                gcBean.getName(), gcBean.getCollectionCount(), gcBean.getCollectionTime());
        }
    }
    
    public MemoryUsageSnapshot takeSnapshot() {
        Runtime runtime = Runtime.getRuntime();
        return MemoryUsageSnapshot.builder()
            .totalMemory(runtime.totalMemory())
            .freeMemory(runtime.freeMemory())
            .maxMemory(runtime.maxMemory())
            .usedMemory(runtime.totalMemory() - runtime.freeMemory())
            .timestamp(Instant.now())
            .build();
    }
}
```

## Code Review Checklist for WoS Bot Memory Management

### Critical Issues (🚨)
- [ ] All AutoCloseable resources are properly closed with try-with-resources (ADB connections, OCR resources)
- [ ] ThreadLocal variables are cleaned up to prevent memory leaks (ProfileLogger, user context)
- [ ] Screenshot cache has size limits and eviction policy
- [ ] ADB device cache has TTL and proper invalidation
- [ ] OCR settings are reused (CommonOCRSettings) instead of creating new instances
- [ ] Static collections are not growing unboundedly (caches, task queues)
- [ ] Service singletons properly manage resources and cleanup on shutdown
- [ ] Task queues are stopped and cleared when profiles are removed

### Performance Issues (⚡)
- [ ] Collections have appropriate initial capacity when size is known (task queues, profile lists)
- [ ] Appropriate collection types chosen based on usage patterns (ConcurrentHashMap for caches)
- [ ] StringBuilder used for string concatenation in loops (log messages, OCR text processing)
- [ ] Screenshot pixel data handled efficiently (DTORawImage with proper byte array management)
- [ ] OCR regions are precisely defined (smaller regions = less memory usage)
- [ ] Image processing operations release resources properly (OpenCV Mat objects)
- [ ] Stream operations use limit() for large datasets (task lists, log messages)
- [ ] Parallel streams used judiciously (not recommended for OCR/ADB operations due to thread safety)

### Best Practice Issues (🔧)
- [ ] Proper GC algorithm chosen for application characteristics
- [ ] JVM heap and memory parameters are tuned appropriately
- [ ] Object pooling used for expensive object creation
- [ ] String interning used only for constants, not user input
- [ ] Memory monitoring and alerting implemented
- [ ] Direct memory usage monitored for NIO applications

## Implementation Guidelines for WoS Bot

When generating Java code:
1. Always use try-with-resources for AutoCloseable objects (ADB connections, file streams)
2. Set appropriate initial capacity for collections when size is known (task queues, profile lists)
3. Choose appropriate collection types based on access patterns (ConcurrentHashMap for caches)
4. Implement proper ThreadLocal cleanup (ProfileLogger, user context in tasks)
5. Limit cache sizes (screenshot cache, device cache) and implement eviction policies
6. Reuse OCR settings objects (CommonOCRSettings) instead of creating new instances
7. Cache screenshots per emulator but limit cache size and implement TTL
8. Use StringBuilder for string concatenation operations (log messages, OCR text)
9. Implement proper resource cleanup in service shutdown methods
10. Monitor memory usage for OCR operations and screenshot processing
11. Clean up task queues and caches when profiles are removed
12. Use appropriate JVM tuning for desktop application (consider heap size for OCR operations)
13. Handle large image data efficiently (DTORawImage pixel arrays)
14. Implement proper cleanup for ADB bridge and device connections
